== Bleisure Planning: A Practical Guide

Cost neutrality, stated as a principle in this chapter, becomes workable only through habits at booking time. The guidance below describes how experienced travelers structure a combined trip so that the business and personal portions separate cleanly on the folio, in the diary, and in the minds of everyone downstream who reads them. None of it alters any eligibility outcome; it exists so that the ones who qualify are easy to process and the ones who do not are easy to settle privately.

Book the personal portion through personal channels wherever possible. A reservation made on a private card generates a folio that needs no line removed afterwards, which is faster and less error-prone than carving lines out after the fact. Where a single booking is unavoidable, split it at the boundary: note the date on which the business purpose ended, and expect Finance Shared Services to carve the folio accordingly. Keep the personal days in your own holiday ledger, not the engagement calendar; a leisure afternoon that appears on a client-facing timeline invites questions no one enjoys answering.

#card-do("Mark the boundary date in the submission note")[
  A clean note states when the business purpose ended and when the personal extension began, in one sentence, with the boundary date unmistakable. Reviewers process such submissions quickly, and the traveler's own recollection benefits from the same clarity months later.
]

=== The Cost-Neutrality Review Worksheet

Travelers planning a personal extension complete a one-page worksheet, available from the Travel Desk intranet page, before ticketing. The worksheet exists to make the cost-neutrality review mechanical rather than argumentative, and to give reviewers a consistent document to attach to the file. It asks for the following, in prose fields rather than calculations:

- *Business end date and personal start date*, stated separately, so the boundary is explicit rather than inferred.
- *A fare comparison statement*: the traveler confirms in writing that the combined ticket did not cost more than the business-only itinerary would have, and identifies the comparison basis used.
- *A folio plan*: how lodging and other charges for the personal days will be kept separate, paid privately, or carved out at settlement.
- *A leave record reference*: the matching entry in the firm's holiday system, so People Operations sees the personal days as leave.
- *Signatures*: the traveler and the approving Practice Leader.

The worksheet travels with the expense submission as an attachment. A missing worksheet does not change the outcome of any eligibility rule; it merely delays processing until one is produced, which review experience suggests is a strong enough incentive on its own.

== Travel Insurance and Disruption Guidance

The firm maintains business travel insurance covering professionals while traveling on firm business. Coverage is arranged centrally by People Operations, and the policy summary is available on the intranet alongside the emergency assistance line. Personal extension days fall outside the business journey, and professionals who want cover for the personal portion arrange it privately; this is the same cost-neutrality principle applied to insurance rather than to folios.

Disruption is handled as a process, not a negotiation. The table below records the expected immediate action, the party to notify, and the documentation to retain for each common disruption type. In every case, the reimbursement treatment of the affected night or journey follows the ordinary chapters of this handbook; the guidance here concerns safety, notification, and records, not eligibility.

